{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-32893",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16+{t}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(16+{t}^{3})\\,j & =c(i+3{t}^{2}\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 16+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 16 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =2\\,i+(16+{2}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+24\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(2,24).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
